+## Building and installing the host software and Python bindings
+
+The host-side part of this module (the `librfnoc-oot-blocks` block controller
+library, headers, the `rfnoc_oot_blocks` Python module and the examples) is
+built with CMake. The Python bindings are **optional and silently skipped** if
+their build dependencies are not found, so check the CMake output carefully.
+
+### Prerequisites
+
+- UHD 4.10 built **with the Python API** and installed (the default UHD build
+  does this; `uhd_config_info --enabled-components` must list
+  `LibUHD - Python API`). `python3 -c "import uhd; print(uhd.__version__)"`
+  must print exactly the version that `uhd_config_info --version` reports,
+  otherwise CMake refuses to enable the Python API for this module.
+- pybind11 CMake package and headers. On Ubuntu 22.04:
+
+  ```sh
+  sudo apt install pybind11-dev python3-pybind11 python3-dev python3-numpy
+  ```
+
+  The pybind11 version must be ABI-compatible with the pybind11 that UHD was
+  built against (UHD 4.10 bundles pybind11 2.10, "internals v4"). Ubuntu 22.04's
+  `pybind11-dev` 2.9.1 shares that ABI and has been verified to work. Do **not**
+  use pybind11 3.x from pip; it uses a newer internals ABI and the module will
+  fail to resolve `uhd.rfnoc.noc_block_base` at import time.
+
+### Build and install
+
+```sh
+cd /path/to/rfnoc-oot-blocks
+cmake -S . -B build        # add -DUHD_FPGA_DIR=/path/to/uhd/fpga to also get FPGA targets
+make -C build -j$(nproc)
+sudo make -C build install
+```
+
+In the `cmake` output, confirm these two lines are present:
+
+```
+-- Enabling Python API for this module.
+-- Installing 'rfnoc_oot_blocks' Python module to: /usr/local/lib/python3.10/site-packages
+```
+
+If they are missing, pybind11 or the `uhd` Python module was not found.
+Install the missing dependency and **re-run CMake with a fresh cache**, because
+`ENABLE_PYTHON_API` is a cached variable and keeps its `FALSE` value once it
+has been computed:
+
+```sh
+rm -rf build/CMakeCache.txt build/CMakeFiles   # or: cmake -S . -B build -DENABLE_PYTHON_API=ON
+cmake -S . -B build
+```
+
+### Verify
+
+```sh
+python3 -c "from rfnoc_oot_blocks import AuroraBlockControl, channel_stop_policy; print(AuroraBlockControl)"
+```
+
+This should print `<class 'rfnoc_oot_blocks.rfnoc_oot_blocks_python.aurora_block_control'>`.
+If you get `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'uhd'` (or `rfnoc_oot_blocks`),
+note that UHD and this module are installed to
+`/usr/local/lib/python3.10/site-packages`, which Ubuntu's system Python does
+not search by default. Add it to your environment:
+
+```sh
+export PYTHONPATH=/usr/local/lib/python3.10/site-packages   # e.g. in ~/.bashrc
+```
+
+### Using the bindings
+
+```python
+import uhd.rfnoc
+from rfnoc_oot_blocks import AuroraBlockControl, channel_stop_policy
+
+graph = uhd.rfnoc.RfnocGraph("addr=192.168.20.2")
+aurora = AuroraBlockControl(graph.get_block("0/Aurora#0"))
+print(aurora.get_status().link_status)
+```
+
+The Python examples in `examples/` (installed to
+`/usr/local/share/uhd/examples/rfnoc-oot-blocks/`) use the bindings the same way.
+
